dict(pas="3.1", w="孔子/Kongzi — Confucius | 謂/said of, spoke about | 季氏：「=jì shì/the Ji — the Jisun house of Lu//Ji (the clan)//clan, house | ^八=bā/eight | 佾=yì/rows (of dancers) | 舞=wǔ/dancing | 於/in | 庭，=tíng/(his) courtyard | ^是/this | 可/can | 忍=rěn/to bear to do, bring oneself to | 也，/(assertion) | ^孰=shú/what, which thing | 不/not | 可/can | 忍=rěn/be borne, brought oneself to | 也？」/(question)",
  l="Kongzi said of the Ji: “Eight rows dancing in his courtyard —\nif this he can bring himself to,\nwhat can he not bring himself to?”",
  i="Confucius said of the head of the Ji house: “He has eight rows of dancers performing in his courtyard. If he can bring himself to do this, there is nothing he will stop at.”",
  n="The book takes its name from 八佾 'eight rows' in its first line; Zhu Xi notes that all its 26 passages, with the last two of Book 2, concern the rites and music. 季氏 is the Jisun 季孫 house, the strongest of the three great families of Lu; He Yan's commentator Ma Rong names its head as Ji Huanzi. 佾 yì is a row of dancers. Zhu Xi: the Son of Heaven had eight rows, a feudal lord six, a grandee four, an officer two, each row with as many dancers as there were rows (so 8 × 8 = 64); he also records the view that every row had eight, which is He Yan's Ma Rong's, and says he cannot tell which is right. A grandee using the king's dance is a usurpation. 謂 here is 'to speak of (someone)', not 'to call'. The last line turns on 忍 'to bear': Zhu Xi takes it as 'to bring oneself to do' — if he can do this, what can he not do? — and records the other sense, 'to tolerate': if this can be tolerated, what cannot? He Yan's Ma Rong glosses 孰 as 誰 'who'. Zhu Xi quotes Fan Zuyu: the numbers step down by twos, and not a hair's breadth of usurpation is allowed between them."),
