dict(pas="3.3", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^人/a man | 而/and yet | 不/not | 仁，/humane | ^如/what (to do) with | 禮/the rites | 何？/(question: 如…何 'what about…?') | ^人/a man | 而/and yet | 不/not | 仁，/humane | ^如/what with | 樂=yuè/music | 何？」/(question)",
  l="The Master said: “A man, and yet not humane —\nwhat of the rites?\nA man, and yet not humane —\nwhat of music?”",
  i="The Master said: “If a man lacks humaneness, the rites will not answer to him. If a man lacks humaneness, music will not answer to him.”",
  n="人而不仁: 而 between subject and predicate again gives 'a man, and yet not humane' (as 2.22 人而無信). 如…何 wraps round its object: 如禮何 'what about the rites, what can be done with them?' 樂 is yuè 'music' here. Zhu Xi quotes You Zuo: if a man is not humane his human heart is lost, and though he wants to use rites and music, they will not serve him. He also quotes Master Cheng — humaneness is the right order of the world; lose it and there is neither order nor ease — and a Mr Li, who suspects the editors set the saying here, after 3.1 and 3.2, because it was aimed at the families usurping the rites and music."),
